editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:17:"A drawing can be found," said Seln. "Beans can be eaten." He laid a second bean beside the first, a hand's breadth to its left. "Nine days ago, a query at the provincial registry that keeps the Velmere estate's file. The standing of the household. The tiers of the people in it. The estate's registered relationships." A third, to the left again. "Twelve days ago, a query at the district seat. The certification history of a Wind practitioner, Iron Rank One, of this house, *for completeness of the respondent institution's file*." And a fourth, at the far left of the row. "Three weeks ago, at this house's own counter, a request from a district office for the sponsorship papers, *for the proceedings' record*. The fourth item in it asked for a schedule of every tie this house has to the enrollee. Sponsorship. Floor hours. The standing linked to his evaluation. His travel endorsements." He touched that bean. "The registrar answered in full, and said in his covering letter that he had answered in full, twice. That is the registrar."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:131:"And then nothing at all." Seln took his finger back. "You won't find this part in any code. No office has ever needed it written down. A finished inventory isn't an instrument. Nobody serves it. Nobody publishes it. It has no title. It is five files in five drawers in five offices, every one of them lawful, every one as dull as a laundry list, and all five indexed under one name." He squared the four beans again with the side of his finger, and did not touch the fifth. "If the seat rules for the house, the five drawers are never opened together. In time each office weeds its old papers, as offices do, and not one person anywhere will have broken a single rule. If the seat rules the other way, it takes one clerk one afternoon to fetch the five files to one desk. Then every gate shuts on the same morning. And the enrollee finds out what that sixth week was for on a morning when there is nothing left to do about it."
