editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:127:The kind pile was always the tallest, and the strangest. Tonight it had a letter from a barge-master's widow on the lower Ost, who had watched the Norhold match from the top tier and wished him to know that the whole river was on his side, though she could not speak for the fish. It had four close pages from a man who had washed out of a hill academy at Copper twenty years ago, who had read the Greyvane transcript and wished to explain, at length and with diagrams, that he too had walked a corridor, and how the doors in his had been arranged. It had a drover from the north road who had stood on the Concourse's top step for the whole of the match and wanted it entered somewhere that his knees had held.
