+# Editorial review — Movement 3
+
+## Coverage and review mode
+
+Fresh-context simulated editorial read of all seven supplied chapters, 14–20, in order. This is an informed continuity/story and formula pass using the supplied canon, not a blind cold read and not real human audience testing.
+
+**Disposition:** focused repair, then targeted recheck. The movement is structurally successful and needs no scene-level rewrite. One forward-continuity defect is mandatory to resolve; the rhythm issue merits only a restrained manual pass.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest catches immediately on the late pigeons and the courtesy copy that asks Vastin nothing. Chapter 14 turns a procedural omission into a personal wound, and the tab *Pending a proceeding worth sending it to* is the movement's first major landing. The pull strengthens through the visitors' gallery, Daeva's fifteen words, Seln's beans, Hesk's letter, and the two-part ruling.
+
+Interest slips slightly in the middle of chapters 16 and 19, where several separate offices, queries, files, and timelines must be retained at once. The concrete devices—the baskets, piles, beans, flour sack, steel rule, and lot number—mostly prevent that material from becoming abstract. Chapter 20 is long, but the honest-floor scene supplies body and discovery before the hearing, and *Both* gives the legal sequence a clear emotional answer.
+
+Cael remains the person whose changing understanding matters most: he moves from counting whether the system is correct to counting what it can close. Vastin is the movement's strongest secondary attachment because his integrity costs him influence without turning him into an ally. I expect the next movement to stop asking whether Halcenvane can win on paper and start asking what the five will do after the paper wins and the institution proceeds anyway. I would continue.
+
+## Reader lenses
+
+Scores are editorial judgments, not calibrated audience measurements. Confidence refers to the judgment, not to demographic prediction.
+
+| Dimension | Exact passage location | Fluent thirteen-year-old | Adult genre reader | Reason and confidence |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| Opening pull | Ch. 14, opening through *For the evaluator's file, per standing distribution* | 8 | 9 | The late pigeons create a small wrongness before the document supplies the threat. Vastin's deliberate pace may delay the hook for a younger reader, but the omission of a question is quickly legible. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Keep reading | Ch. 20, ruling from *Having heard both parties* through *Both at once. You are lawful. The machine goes on.* | 9 | 9 | Each chapter pays a local question while enlarging the corridor. The preliminary ruling is both satisfying and damaging, then Seln's last line turns it forward. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Interest / freshness | Ch. 19, the five beans and “walking the fences”; Ch. 20, lot seventeen | 8 | 9 | Bureaucratic coercion becomes physical and countable without requiring a visible villain. The younger lens may need more effort to retain which office owns which file. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Clarity / flow | Ch. 16, fee-office query through the four-name column; Ch. 19, Ternhall letter through the inventory explanation | 7 | 8 | Scene-to-scene causality is clean, but the accumulation of offices, dates, and kinds of standing is the movement's main one-pass burden. Repeated concrete objects restore orientation. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Character attachment | Ch. 14, Vastin's candle and unnamed addressee; Ch. 16, the schoolroom letter; Ch. 20, Cael choosing lot seventeen | 9 | 9 | Agency and vulnerability are expressed through choices rather than pleas. Cael, Vastin, Lira, Brom, Karis, and Seln remain recognizably different. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Humor / warmth | Ch. 15, Ephram confiscating the bad woodcut; Ch. 16, *Kind. Unkind. Unhinged.* and Brom's “good circle”; Ch. 18, Hesk/Vell/Daeva imagined in one room | 8 | 8 | Humor grows from affection and trade-specific habits and never erases the cost. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Action / suspense | Ch. 17, all four exchanges under the gallery; Ch. 20, Lira reading the lane by its dust-shadow | 8 | 8 | The Current adapts twice, Cael must win inside a false presentation of himself, and every expenditure is trackable. The private-floor scene converts the lost daylight into learning rather than consolation. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Progression payoff | Ch. 17, the deliberately small second burst; Ch. 20, six bursts, the opened give, one lane, and the ten-count bill | 8 | 8 | Capability, concealment, cost, and learning are all visible. No new fragment is needed because the payoff is improved use and Lira's new perception. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Connection | Ch. 15, Brom reading Seln's sentence back onto Seln; Ch. 19, Hesk's letter completing Karis's blank line; Ch. 20, Seln's closing line | 8 | 9 | Plants and relationships carry strongly from prior movements and across this one. The score is held below 9 for the Velmere-letter forward-continuity conflict. **Confidence: high.** |
+| Read-aloud quality | Ch. 15, counsel/Withrow/Karis sequence; Ch. 20, Jent and the counsel before the hood | 8 | 8 | Voices are distinct and attribution is reliable. A few long explanatory paragraphs ask the listener to retain several nested referents, but the decisive lines are clean. **Confidence: medium-high.** |
+
+## Story and continuity
+
+### What works
+
+- The movement delivers the planned escalation in the correct order: Vastin is refused; public attention becomes an economy; the cost reaches the circle; the service-side pattern reaches Denvash; the house wins validity and still proceeds toward re-evaluation.
+- Cause and effect remain legible. The observers cause Rooke to close the floor; that costs Lira her useful daylight; the honest floor then lets her discover the lane's shadow under lamps. The new learning is an earned consequence, not compensation that makes the loss fair.
+- The recurring instrument metaphor develops rather than repeats. Vastin writes a finding with no proceeding; Umber refuses added meaning; Daeva files witness testimony; Jent withdraws his own false overreach; the seat can use every honest product without listening to its maker.
+- The whole-chapter Vastin cutaway is purposeful and stays inside his allowed knowledge. He notices the Kindling mismatch but gives it no theory. He learns nothing about the cache, integration, the sub-layer, or the faceless faction.
+- Jent remains especially effective. His correction of his own overstatement in chapter 20 prevents the conflict from collapsing into good people versus liars and makes Cael's search for the unseen “handler” credible.
+
+### Continuity finding
+
+**Velmere letter — confirmed forward-state conflict.** In chapter 16, the letter that arrives fourteen days after the day-31 letter is slit open and read at the table. This is the second new Velmere letter of the book. The book state says two more arrive in Book 6, while `BOOK_MAP` §4e #10 reserves the second letter of the year as the one still unopened four weeks before the ruling in Movement 7. As written, Movement 7 would require an unplanned third letter or would lose the unopened-letter beat. This is a plan/ledger conflict, not a contradiction inside these seven chapters. The clean repair is to let this same chapter-16 letter remain sealed and carry forward.
+
+### Canon checks
+
+- **Calendar:** coherent. The sequence from day 41 through the third sitting on day 54 preserves the sixth-week inventory statement. No season or month name is introduced.
+- **Bodies:** coherent. Lira's left arm is carried close from old habit, not current injury. Cael's struck forearm throbs on the day of the visitors' bout and is not implausibly treated as a lasting wound five days later. The private-floor knockdown has no invented injury.
+- **Power limits:** coherent. The public band uses two bursts, one late spark, and the give shut; the private floor reaches six bursts on boards, opens the give, lays one lane, and pays the established ten-count delay. Shadow is neither used nor explained. No new fragment or tier appears.
+- **Knowledge and reserved disclosures:** pass. Falsification is planted only through the junior's mismatch and the inventory's shape; it is not named. `[UNBOUND]`, the Architect, the Quieting, Tide, and the cache's contents remain absent. Daeva's private letter remains Cael's; Lira knows only that it came. Seln is not told why Shadow was sealed, correctly preserving the Movement 6 reveal and “You kept my file too, then.”
+- **Roles, ranks, and names:** pass. Vastin's age is not stated and his forty-year career is used. Seln remains the teaching assistant at the copying table while a separate desk clerk serves the counter. Withrow is Chancellor; the counsel remains unnamed; the Current opponent is Iron Rank Four; Daeva is Gold Rank Three.
+- **Reader Standard:** pass. Language is clean; violence is non-gory, trackable, and consequential; there is no sexual content or innuendo. Honesty, loyalty, restraint, care, and moral cost remain the center of the movement, while institutional wrong is clearly named as wrong even when it succeeds procedurally.
+
